<!DOCTYPE topic
        SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="AZD: AI assistant" id="Azd-AI">

    <primary-label ref="azd"/>
    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>


    <link-summary>What the AZD AI assistant does, which data it sends where and how to configure AI providers, including GitHub Copilot, and prompts.</link-summary>
    <card-summary>Use AI in AZD with the built-in provider, your own OpenAI, Claude, Gemini or Ollama models, or your GitHub Copilot plan.</card-summary>
    <web-summary>The AZD AI assistant for Azure DevOps in JetBrains IDEs: built-in provider, privacy,
        editable prompts, your own OpenAI, Claude, Gemini or Ollama models, and GitHub Copilot.</web-summary>

    <p>
        AZD uses AI to write pull request text and commit messages, review pull requests and explain pipeline logs.
        It works right after installation with the built-in AZD provider.
        You can add your own providers (OpenAI, Claude, Gemini, a local Ollama server or GitHub Copilot) and edit the prompts
        in <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD | AI Settings</ui-path>.
    </p>
    <deflist type="medium">
        <def title="Write">
            Pull request titles and descriptions, updated descriptions and commit messages;
            see <a href="AI-Title-Description-Generation.topic"/>.
        </def>
        <def title="Review">
            AI Chat about a pull request, the guided AI Auto Review and AI draft comments;
            see <a href="AI-Pull-Request-Review.topic"/>.
            To polish a comment, see <a href="Azd-PR-Comments.topic" anchor="polish-comment"/>.
        </def>
        <def title="Act">
            With MCP tools, AI Chat can read and change work items, pull requests and pipelines;
            see <a href="AI-Pull-Request-Review.topic" anchor="mcp-tools"/>.
        </def>
        <def title="Explain pipelines">
            Chat about a pipeline log;
            see <a href="Azure-DevOps-Pipelines.topic" anchor="analyze-logs-with-ai"/>.
        </def>
    </deflist>

    <chapter title="Data and privacy" id="ai-privacy">
        <p>
            AI features send the text they work on, such as commit messages, diffs, pull request descriptions and comments, to the AI provider you select.
        </p>
        <list>
            <li>
                The built-in <control>AZD</control> provider runs a GPT model deployed on the plugin author's Azure subscription.
                No data is stored or collected; the endpoint only serves each request and response.
            </li>
            <li>
                With OpenAI, Claude or Gemini, the data goes to that service under your own API key and its terms.
            </li>
            <li>
                With GitHub Copilot, the data goes to GitHub through the Copilot CLI, under your Copilot plan and its terms.
            </li>
            <li>
                With Ollama or another server on your machine or local network, the data stays on that server.
            </li>
        </list>
        <p>
            While the AZD provider is active, AZD shows the notification <control>AZD AI uses a cloud model by default</control> once per IDE session.
            Click <control>Open AI settings</control> to change the provider, or <control>Don't show again</control> to hide the notification.
            For details, see the <a href="privacy-policy.topic">privacy policy</a>.
        </p>
        <!-- planned-image src="azd_ai_default_notice.png" alt="Notification that AZD AI uses a cloud model by default, with Open AI settings and Don't show again" -->
        <p>
            To stop sending data to the AZD provider, add and enable another provider, or turn AI off; see <a anchor="enable-ai"/>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Turn AI features on or off" id="enable-ai">
        <procedure title="Turn AI assistance on or off" id="toggle-ai">
            <step>
                Open the <control>Settings</control> dialog (<shortcut>Ctrl+Alt+S</shortcut>, macOS: <shortcut>⌘ ,</shortcut>)
                and select <ui-path>Version Control | AZD | AI Settings</ui-path>.
            </step>
            <step>
                Select or clear <control>Enable AI assistance</control>.
                AI is on by default.
                <img src="azd_ai_settings.png" alt="AZD AI Settings page with Enable AI assistance selected and the AI Providers table" width="706" border-effect="rounded" thumbnail="true"/>
            </step>
            <step>
                Click <control>OK</control>.
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p>
            When AI is off, AZD hides the AI generation buttons and the <control>Review with AI</control> item of the right-click menus.
            The <control>Pull Request Actions</control> (⋮) menu still lists <control>Review with AI</control>, but its <control>AI Chat</control> items are disabled.
            The page also has these options:
        </p>
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Enable MCP Tools">
                Lets AI Chat act on Azure DevOps through MCP tools.
                Off by default; see <a href="AI-Pull-Request-Review.topic" anchor="mcp-tools"/>.
            </def>
            <def title="Show illustrations in AI review tour">
                Shows a cover image on the first card of AI Auto Review; see <a href="AI-Pull-Request-Review.topic" anchor="ai-auto-review"/>.
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Configure AI providers" id="ai-providers">
        <p>
            The <control>AI Providers</control> table on the <control>AI Settings</control> page lists the configured providers with their <control>Provider</control>, <control>Model</control> and <control>Enabled</control> state.
            On first start, the table has one row: the <control>AZD</control> provider with the AZD GPT-5.4 Mini model, enabled and active.
        </p>
        <p>
            Enabled providers appear in the provider lists of the AI buttons and menus.
            The provider you select there becomes the active one.
            Selecting the <control>Enabled</control> checkbox in the table also makes that provider active.
        </p>
        <procedure title="Add an AI provider" id="add-ai-provider">
            <step>
                In <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD | AI Settings</ui-path>, click <control>+</control> above the <control>AI Providers</control> table.
            </step>
            <step>
                In the <control>Add AI Provider</control> dialog, select the <control>Provider</control> and the <control>Model</control>,
                and fill in the fields that the provider needs; see <a anchor="ai-provider-fields"/>.
                <img src="azd_ai_add_provider.png" alt="Add AI Provider dialog with Ollama selected, a model, the default local API URL and the Fetch Models button" width="489" border-effect="rounded"/>
            </step>
            <step>
                Click <control>Test Connection</control>.
            </step>
            <step>
                Select <control>Enabled</control> and click <control>OK</control>.
                If you enable a provider without a successful test, AZD asks whether to continue; click <control>Test First</control> or <control>Continue</control>.
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p>The <control>Add AI Provider</control> and <control>Edit AI Provider</control> dialogs have these fields:</p>
        <deflist type="medium" id="ai-provider-fields">
            <def title="Provider">
                <control>AZD</control>, <control>OpenAI</control>, <control>Claude</control>, <control>Gemini</control>, <control>Ollama</control> or <control>GitHub Copilot</control>.
            </def>
            <def title="Model">
                The models that AZD knows for the provider, newest first; see <a anchor="ai-models"/>.
                You can also type a model name.
                For Ollama, click <control>Fetch Models</control> to list the models installed on the server.
                For GitHub Copilot, the list shows the models of your Copilot plan.
            </def>
            <def title="API Key">
                Your key for the provider.
                The <control>AZD</control> and <control>Ollama</control> providers do not need one.
                For <control>GitHub Copilot</control>, the field is hidden; you sign in with GitHub instead.
                OpenAI, Claude and Gemini need one for their official endpoints, but not for a custom URL on <code>localhost</code> or a private network.
                AZD stores keys in the IDE password safe.
            </def>
            <def title="API URL">
                Leave it blank to use the provider's default URL.
                Enter a URL to use a proxy or a compatible server, for example <code>http://localhost:11434</code> for Ollama.
                For an OpenAI-compatible server, select the <control>OpenAI</control> provider.
                You can enter the URL with or without <code>/v1</code> at the end, for example <code>http://localhost:1234/v1</code> for LM Studio.
                For <control>GitHub Copilot</control>, the field is hidden.
            </def>
            <def title="Enabled">
                Makes the provider available in the AI buttons and menus.
            </def>
            <def title="Test Connection">
                Sends a test request with the current values.
                If the test fails, the message names the cause, for example <control>Authentication failed. Please check your API key.</control>
                or <control>Your API key's plan has no quota for this model</control>.
            </def>
        </deflist>
        <p>
            To edit, remove or reorder providers, select a row and use the buttons above the table.
        </p>

        <chapter title="Available models" id="ai-models">
            <p>
                The <control>Model</control> list shows the newest models first.
                When you add a provider, AZD selects the first one, marked <emphasis>selected</emphasis> below.
            </p>
            <table>
                <tr>
                    <td>Provider</td>
                    <td>Models</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><control>AZD</control></td>
                    <td><code>GPT-5.4 Mini (Azure)</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><control>OpenAI</control></td>
                    <td><code>gpt-5.6-sol</code> (selected), <code>gpt-5.6-terra</code>, <code>gpt-5.6-luna</code>, <code>gpt-5.5-pro</code>, <code>gpt-5.5</code> and older GPT models</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><control>Claude</control></td>
                    <td><code>claude-fable-5</code> (selected), <code>claude-opus-5</code> and older Opus models, <code>claude-sonnet-5</code> and older Sonnet models, <code>claude-haiku-4-5</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><control>Gemini</control></td>
                    <td><code>gemini-3.7-flash</code> (selected), followed by older Gemini models such as <code>gemini-3.5-flash</code> and <code>gemini-3.1-pro-preview</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><control>Ollama</control></td>
                    <td>
                        Common open models, with <code>gpt-oss:20b</code> selected.
                        Click <control>Fetch Models</control> to list the models installed on your server.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><control>GitHub Copilot</control></td>
                    <td>
                        The models of your Copilot plan, each with its usage multiplier, for example <code>Claude Sonnet 5 · 1x</code>.
                        AZD selects your plan's default model.
                    </td>
                </tr>
            </table>
        </chapter>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Use GitHub Copilot" id="copilot-provider">
        <p>
            The <control>GitHub Copilot</control> provider uses your existing Copilot plan, so you need no API key.
            AZD sends requests through the GitHub Copilot CLI, which runs in the background.
            Each request counts toward the usage of your Copilot plan.
        </p>
        <p>Before you start, make sure that:</p>
        <list>
            <li>Your GitHub account has a Copilot plan.</li>
            <li>
                If your Copilot plan comes from an organization, the organization allows the Copilot CLI.
            </li>
            <li>
                The Copilot CLI is installed on the machine that runs the IDE, for example with
                <code>brew install copilot-cli</code> on macOS, <code>winget install GitHub.Copilot</code> on Windows,
                or <code>npm install -g @github/copilot</code>.
            </li>
        </list>
        <procedure title="Add GitHub Copilot as a provider" id="add-copilot-provider">
            <step>
                In <ui-path>Settings | Version Control | AZD | AI Settings</ui-path>, click <control>+</control> above the <control>AI Providers</control> table.
            </step>
            <step>
                In the <control>Add AI Provider</control> dialog, select <control>GitHub Copilot</control> in <control>Provider</control>.
                AZD looks for the CLI and checks your sign-in.
                <!-- planned-image src="azd_ai_add_copilot.png" alt="Add AI Provider dialog with GitHub Copilot selected, the Copilot CLI found, the account signed in and a model of the Copilot plan" -->
            </step>
            <step>
                Check the <control>Copilot CLI</control> row.
                If it says that the CLI was not found, it lists install commands with <control>Copy</control> links.
                Install the CLI, then click <control>Check Again</control>.
            </step>
            <step>
                If the <control>Account</control> row shows <control>Not signed in</control>, click <control>Sign in with GitHub</control>.
                The <control>Sign in to GitHub Copilot</control> dialog shows a code and a page.
                Click <control>Copy Code and Open Browser</control>, paste the code on the GitHub page and authorize the CLI.
                The dialog closes when you are signed in, and the <control>Account</control> row shows <control>Signed in</control>.
            </step>
            <step>
                Select a <control>Model</control>.
                Models that your plan or organization has not enabled are listed below the <control>Account</control> row, after <control>Not enabled for your account or organization</control>.
            </step>
            <step>
                Click <control>Test Connection</control>.
                On success, AZD shows <control>Connected to GitHub Copilot</control> with the model name.
            </step>
            <step>
                Select <control>Enabled</control> and click <control>OK</control>.
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p>
            If AZD cannot start the sign-in, it shows a command to run in a terminal.
            Copy it, run it, and then click <control>Refresh</control>.
        </p>
        <p>
            In AI Chat with MCP tools turned on, Copilot can use only the Azure DevOps MCP tools that AZD provides;
            see <a href="AI-Pull-Request-Review.topic" anchor="mcp-tools"/>.
        </p>
        <deflist type="medium" id="copilot-troubleshooting">
            <def title="Copilot sign-in expired">
                Edit the <control>GitHub Copilot</control> provider in the <control>AI Providers</control> table and sign in again.
            </def>
            <def title="Copilot request limit reached">
                Your plan has used up its requests for the selected model.
                Edit the provider and select a model with a lower multiplier.
            </def>
            <def title="Copilot CLI is disabled for your account or organization">
                Ask your organization to allow the Copilot CLI.
                A policy change can take up to 30 minutes to apply.
            </def>
            <def title="Your GitHub account has no Copilot access">
                Sign in with a GitHub account that has a Copilot plan.
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Configure prompts" id="configure-prompts">
        <p>
            Expand <control>Configure Prompts</control> on the <control>AI Settings</control> page.
            Select a prompt on the left to edit its text on the right.
            Clearing the checkbox of a prompt makes its text read-only.
            For some prompts it also turns off the features that use them, as listed below.
        </p>
        <img src="azd_ai_prompts.png" alt="Configure Prompts section with PR Code Review selected and its prompt text in the editor" width="706" border-effect="rounded" thumbnail="true"/>
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Pull Request title prompt">
                Generates pull request titles.
                Placeholders: <code>{commits}</code>, <code>{diffs}</code>.
                Clearing the checkbox only makes the prompt read-only; title generation stays available.
            </def>
            <def title="Pull Request Description Prompt">
                Generates and updates pull request descriptions.
                Placeholders: <code>{existingDescription}</code>, <code>{commits}</code>, <code>{diffs}</code>.
                Clearing the checkbox hides the description AI buttons.
            </def>
            <def title="Commit message prompt">
                Generates commit messages.
                Placeholders: <code>{workItem}</code>, <code>{diffs}</code>.
                Clearing the checkbox hides the commit message AI button.
            </def>
            <def title="PR Code Review">
                Not used by the current AI features.
                AI Chat and AI Auto Review use built-in review instructions.
            </def>
            <def title="Diff Comment Review">
                The default prompt of the <control>Generate AI Code Review</control> dialog.
                Placeholder: <code>{diff}</code>.
                Clearing the checkbox removes AI Auto Review from the right-click menu,
                and turns off the AI draft comments button and the AI button in comment editors.
            </def>
        </deflist>
        <p>
            Keep the placeholders in your text; AZD replaces them with the data of the current pull request or commit.
        </p>
        <note>
            <p>
                Changes under <control>Configure Prompts</control> take effect as you make them, even if you then click <control>Cancel</control>.
                No button restores a default prompt, so copy the text of a prompt before you change it.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

